WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.590
Amani had a battery, a bulb and one wire.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.280
She tried to light the bulb: nothing.

3
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.200
Then she moved the wire, and it lit!

4
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:10.560
What changed?

5
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.140
Let's find out.

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.500
Today: build a circuit that

7
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.400
lights up, and keep it safe.

8
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.750
A circuit is a loop: current

9
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.130
flows out of the battery,

10
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.370
through the bulb, and back in.

11
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.560
A bulb has two contacts, its tip and its

12
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.700
side, and the loop must pass through both.

13
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.820
Amani's first try touched only one.

14
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.370
Think of a bicycle chain.

15
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.420
The pedals push, like the battery.

16
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.900
The moving chain is the current, and

17
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.160
the back wheel is the bulb.

18
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.170
Break the chain anywhere,

19
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:48.890
and everything stops.

20
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.920
To test a material, leave a gap in your

21
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.080
loop and let both ends touch it.

22
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.880
If the bulb lights, it's a conductor:

23
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.500
coins, keys, foil; pencil lead

24
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.520
conducts too, but weakly, so the bulb

25
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.460
glows dimly or not at all.

26
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.570
If not, it's an insulator:

27
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.210
plastic, rubber, paper, dry wood.

28
00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:14.930
Quick check!

29
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.810
Kofi's wires both touch the bulb, but one

30
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.090
end is still inside its plastic coat.

31
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.340
Will the bulb light?

32
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:24.930
No.

33
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.680
Plastic is an insulator, so

34
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.820
the loop is still open.

35
00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.150
Strip the end, and the copper closes it.

36
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.270
That's why wires are

37
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.830
copper inside plastic.

38
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.600
Now swap the bulb for an LED, the light

39
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.140
in phone screens and solar lamps.

40
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.460
It works only one way

41
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.500
round: long leg to plus.

42
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.940
Backwards, it stays dark.

43
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.080
But an LED can't limit its own current.

44
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.650
Straight on a battery, too much

45
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.390
flows, and it burns out.

46
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.480
A resistor in the loop holds the current

47
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.160
down, like a gentle brake on the chain.

48
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.340
How big a resistor?

49
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.540
Two AA batteries give three volts; a red

50
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.300
LED uses about two, leaving one.

51
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.120
For a safe ten milliamps, that

52
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.080
volt needs a hundred ohms.

53
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.480
A bigger resistor gives a dimmer LED.

54
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.690
Never make a short circuit: a wire

55
00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.390
straight from plus to minus.

56
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.000
A huge current flows, and the wire and

57
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.780
battery get hot enough to burn.

58
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.620
And use batteries only: a

59
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:36.780
wall socket can kill.

60
00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:38.790
Your turn.

61
00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.710
Musa holds a bare wire straight

62
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.770
across his battery, to test it.

63
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:44.960
No bulb.

64
00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:46.430
What happens?

65
00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.670
They get hot: that's a short

66
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.370
circuit, so never do it.

67
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.380
Remember: close the loop, test

68
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.860
what conducts, long leg to plus,

69
00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.940
and always a resistor.

70
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.260
Try it yourself: make a tester from two AA

71
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.300
batteries, a torch bulb and kitchen foil.

72
00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.310
Predict, then test ten things at home or

73
00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.190
at school, and sort them into two lists.

74
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.160
Next time, switches that decide.
